  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match hypothyroidism PRS are present. PGS005218 is best supported because its phenotype is aligned to the target (phenotyping_reported: incident hypothyroidism; trait_reported/efo: hypothyroidism) and it comes from a dedicated hypothyroidism GWAS/PRS publication (Nat Genet: “Genome-wide association study and polygenic risk prediction of hypothyroidism.”), with the strongest reported discrimination among candidates in the provided context (full-model AUROC 0.859 with CI, 6 records). Key limitation: the reported AUROC is a full-model metric and the model includes thyroid biomarkers (TSH, T4, anti-TPO) as covariates, which domain guidance flags as potential covariate-driven inflation and not directly comparable to PRS-only performance; PRS-only AUROC/R2 are not provided here, lowering certainty despite strong endpoint alignment and study focus."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002033","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 10 candidates are direct matches by trait_efo=obesity, but they vary in endpoint fidelity and strength of PRS-comparable performance evidence. Several are clearly framework/portability scores (PGS001298 from the UKB ‘813 traits’ pan-trait framework; PGS001825/PGS002033 from the ‘Portability of 245 polygenic scores’ framework), while others have sparse/unclear disease-model metadata (e.g., unknown covariates; GWAS-hits only; or exposure-focused context).

PGS002033 is preferred because it is a direct obesity-related endpoint (‘Overweight, obesity and other hyperalimentation’) and uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (LDpred2) with a very large variant set (846,292), and it reports a PRS-comparable metric (partial correlation 0.0789 with CI) in European evaluation. Within the same publication family and endpoint, it also has stronger reported partial-r than its penalized-regression sibling PGS001825 (0.0547).

Main limitation: the best-supported candidates here are still from a portability/framework study rather than a clearly disease-focused multi-cohort obesity PRS development, and many competing ‘obesity’/ICD-E66/phecode models lack comparable PRS-only performance metrics (AUC/R²) in the provided metadata, preventing a high-confidence selection. Ancestry applicability is also context-dependent (PGS002033 is DEV EUR; EVAL includes multiple ancestries, but the selected validation ancestry is European)."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000053","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All five candidates are direct matches on concept (trait_efo = late-onset Alzheimer's disease). However, the evidence is not strong enough to yield a clearly high-quality winner because PRS-comparable performance is largely missing/heterogeneous and some endpoints/covariates are unclear or potentially non-aligned.

PGS000053 is the best-supported direct-match option from the visible metadata because it has strong endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: familial late-onset Alzheimer's disease), uses basic covariates (age, sex) rather than a risk-wrapper, has a sizable European evaluation sample (n=4,792), and reports an interpretable effect size (OR 1.29, 95% CI 1.21–1.37). Competing models have notable limitations: PGS000054 is evaluated only in AMR (Hispanic/Latin American) despite the target context not specifying ancestry; PGS000334 reports an R²=0.191 with unknown covariates and no effect sizes (and this magnitude is suspicious relative to the provided heritability sanity-check guidance); PGS002289’s phenotyping_reported appears to be a cognition-by-age interaction rather than LOAD case/control; and PGS004918 has very small development/validation samples (n=439 training; n=136 validation) and reports only full-model AUROC (0.731), which the domain guidance says is not a PRS-comparable substitute.

Main limitation: no candidate provides clean, directly comparable PRS-only discrimination metrics (e.g., PRS-only AUROC/R²) for LOAD in a well-described evaluation setting, so the selection relies primarily on endpoint alignment, covariate cleanliness, and limited effect-size reporting."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000327",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Both candidates are direct matches by trait_reported/trait_efo. PGS000327 has stronger endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported is explicitly \"Autism spectrum disorder\"), appears disease-focused (Nat Genet ASD GWAS paper), and reports clearer ASD-relevant performance evidence (R²=0.0245 and OR=1.33 [1.30–1.36]) in EUR evaluation. By contrast, PGS002790 has a phenotype mismatch/ambiguity (phenotyping_reported: \"Psychiatric behavior (withdrawn)\") and only reports a weaker partial R² (~0.0055) without effect sizes, despite a more advanced method (SDPR) and larger SNP count. Main limitation: performance reporting is incomplete for both (no AUC/PGS-only metrics), so preference is driven primarily by endpoint fidelity and the more coherent disease-focused evidence package."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002031",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Two direct-match candidates are present (PGS001823, PGS002031), both targeting the same endpoint (“Disorders of iron metabolism”) with the same EFO mapping (iron metabolism disease), same UKB-based development context, similar covariates, and the same validation setting. With endpoint fidelity effectively tied, the main differentiators in the visible evidence are (i) PRS-comparable performance and (ii) structural/modeling differences. PGS002031 has a slightly higher reported partial correlation in European validation (partial-r 0.1355 [0.1219–0.1492]) than PGS001823 (0.1307 [0.1170–0.1444]) and uses LDpred2 with a larger variant set (6713 vs 654), which is a supportive structural signal when candidates are otherwise near-clones. Limitation: both models come from the same UKB portability framework publication and only report partial-r (no AUC/R2), making the advantage modest and leaving uncertainty about external (non-UKB) generalization; therefore confidence is Moderate rather than High."
}
